       border:1px solid var(--accent); border-radius:999px; padding:.1rem .6rem; }\0a</style>\0a</head>\0a<body>\0a<div class=\22wrap\22>\0a<header>\0a<span class=\22tag\22>clean-server</span>\0a<h1>It serves real pages.</h1>\0a<p class=\22sub\22>Served from a WebAssembly component through the clean:host contract.</p>\0a<nav>\0a<a href=\22/\22>Home</a>\0a<a href=\22/about\22>About</a>\0a<a href=\22/posts/hello-world\22>A post</a>\0a<a href=\22/missing\22>404</a>\0a</nav>\0a</header>\0a<div class=\22card\22>\0a<h2>This is HTML, not a fixture string</h2>\0a<p>The guest is a WebAssembly component. It registered these routes at startup\0athrough <code>clean:host/routing</code> and wrote this document with\0a<code>clean:host/response</code>. The host never parsed a template.</p>\0a</div>\0a<div class=\22card\22>\0a<h2>Routing with parameters</h2>\0a<p>Visit <code>/posts/hello-world</code> &mdash; the slug is captured by the host,\0ahanded to the guest via <code>get-param</code>, and rendered into the page below\0athe title.</p>\0a</div>\0a<div class=\22card\22>\0a<h2>Status codes are the guest's decision</h2>\0a<p>An unmatched path returns a real 404 with its own styled page, because the\0aguest sets the status itself rather than letting the host guess.</p>\0a</div>\0a<footer>clean-server &middot; wasmtime component model &middot; no framework in the request path</footer>\0a</div>\0a</body>\0a</html>\0a")
