    border:1px solid var(--accent); border-radius:999px; padding:.1rem .6rem; }\0a</style>\0a</head>\0a<body>\0a<div class=\22wrap\22>\0a<header>\0a<span class=\22tag\22>clean-server</span>\0a<h1>About this host.</h1>\0a<p class=\22sub\22>Served from a WebAssembly component through the clean:host contract.</p>\0a<nav>\0a<a href=\22/\22>Home</a>\0a<a href=\22/about\22>About</a>\0a<a href=\22/posts/hello-world\22>A post</a>\0a<a href=\22/missing\22>404</a>\0a</nav>\0a</header>\0a<div class=\22card\22>\0a<h2>What clean-server owns</h2>\0a<p>The HTTP surface only: listener, request parsing, response writing, TLS,\0aWebSocket upgrade, and SSE. Composition, pooling, and WASI belong to\0a<code>clean-host-core</code>.</p>\0a</div>\0a<div class=\22card\22>\0a<h2>The contract is a file</h2>\0a<p><code>host.wit</code> is the authoritative declaration of what this host\0aprovides. CI fails when the wasmtime linker and that file disagree, so a guest\0acan trust what it imports.</p>\0a</div>\0a<div class=\22card\22>\0a<h2>Instances are pooled and reset</h2>\0a<p>Each request checks out an instance, runs, and is reset before returning to\0athe pool &mdash; so one request can never observe another's leftover state.</p>\0a</div>\0a<footer>clean-server &middot; wasmtime component model &middot; no framework in the request path</footer>\0a</div>\0a</body>\0a</html>\0a")
